{"title":"Kobayashi dendritic solidification","markdown":{"headingText":"Kobayashi dendritic solidification","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-kobayashi","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n`apps/kobayashi` — coupled phase field and temperature, with anisotropic\nsurface energy. The most physically involved finite-difference application in\nthe set.\n\n## Physical setting\n\nA crystal growing into an undercooled melt. Latent heat released at the moving\ninterface warms the surrounding liquid and suppresses further growth there,\nwhile protrusions reach into colder melt and grow faster. That feedback is the\norigin of dendrites, and the direction they select comes from the anisotropy of\nthe interfacial energy.\n\nTwo-way coupling is essential: the phase field moves the temperature through\nlatent heat, and the temperature moves the phase field through the driving\nforce.\n\n## Governing equations\n\n$$\n\\tau\\,\\partial_t\\phi \\;=\\;\n    \\partial_y\\big(\\epsilon\\,\\epsilon'\\,\\partial_x\\phi\\big)\n  - \\partial_x\\big(\\epsilon\\,\\epsilon'\\,\\partial_y\\phi\\big)\n  + \\epsilon^2\\nabla^2\\phi\n  + \\phi(1-\\phi)\\!\\left(\\phi - \\tfrac12 + M(T)\\right),\n$$ {#eq-kb-phi}\n\n$$\n\\partial_t T \\;=\\; \\nabla^2 T \\;+\\; \\kappa\\,\\partial_t\\phi .\n$$ {#eq-kb-temp}\n\nThe interfacial energy $\\epsilon$ depends on the orientation of the interface\nnormal, and $\\epsilon'$ is its derivative with respect to that angle. The first\ntwo terms of @eq-kb-phi are the anisotropy: they vanish for isotropic\n$\\epsilon$ and are what makes dendrite arms select particular directions rather\nthan growing as a disc.\n\n$M(T)$ is the temperature-dependent driving force, and $\\kappa$ is the latent\nheat coefficient coupling @eq-kb-phi back into @eq-kb-temp.\n\n## Finite-difference formulation\n\nSecond-order centred stencils on a periodic torus in $x$ and $y$. The\nanisotropy terms need the interface normal, so gradients of $\\phi$ are\nevaluated before the divergence-like terms are assembled.\n\nThe latent heat term is applied as\n\n$$\nT^{\\,n+1} \\;=\\; T^{\\,n} + \\Delta t\\,\\nabla^2_h T^{\\,n}\n                 \\;+\\; \\kappa\\big(\\phi^{\\,n+1}-\\phi^{\\,n}\\big),\n$$ {#eq-kb-latent}\n\nthat is, using the *already updated* $\\phi$ rather than a separate time\nderivative. This is a sequential rather than simultaneous update of the two\nfields, and it is deliberate: it matches the reference Julia implementation\nthis application was validated against, so the two can be compared\nstep-for-step.\n\n## Solution strategy\n\nExplicit stepping. Halo exchange for two field groups per step — state and\nauxiliary — unless extended halos are requested, in which case a single wider\nexchange covers both. The GPU drivers keep working arrays resident on device\nand stage $\\phi$ and $T$ to the host only for PNG output and verification.\n\n## Verification\n\n* **Reference agreement.** Checksums pinned against the reference\n  implementation.\n* **CPU–GPU parity.** The CUDA 32²/4-step pin matches the CPU OpenMP pin;\n  `sum_T` differs by 1 ULP.\n* **Multi-rank.** A two-rank variant is registered when MPI suites are on.\n\n## Binaries and inputs\n\n`kobayashi_fd_manual` (MPI halos), `kobayashi_fd_openmp` (torus wrap plus\nOpenMP), plus CUDA and HIP drivers. Command-line driven, with PNG snapshots of\n$\\phi$.\n\n## Scalability\n\nNot measured on LUMI-G. A 1-versus-2-GPU CUDA workflow exists for an H100\npartition, but it is a two-point comparison rather than a strong-scaling curve\nand is not reported here as one.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-